title: What makes an agent expensive is what it remembers
source: hn
url: https://talcaldeg.github.io/articulos/context-economy/
date: '2026-10-13'
authors:
- Tomás Alcalde
why_read: Read this to understand how agent costs are dominated by repeatedly processing accumulated context rather than generating new tokens. You will learn how analyzing session logs reveals the true drivers of LLM token consumption.
hn_id: '50071565'
comments: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50071565
image: /infographics/01-hn-50071565.jpg
---

The true cost driver of autonomous coding agents is not generation, but the compounding tax of re-reading history. An empirical analysis across more than three hundred production agent sessions revealed that ninety-four percent of total token consumption came from re-reading accumulated context. Only zero point eight percent represented tokens the model actually wrote.

Most engineers assume high API bills come from verbose outputs or large tool responses. In practice, long-running agent loops carry a massive quadratic baggage: every prompt turn re-ingests the entire previous conversation, tool outputs, and file diffs.

Mitigating this overhead is not as simple as blindly truncating context mid-task. Early truncation often erases necessary constraints, causing the agent to repeat failed tool invocations and destroy task completion rates. Effective agent harnesses instead require selective summarization, structured memory stores, and aggressive eviction of ephemeral file buffers before history compounds.

If you want to reduce your agent spend, do not optimize output generation; optimize context retention.

